R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10019 - Tracker and Deskbar
Tracker keeps a long name inside the copy window, and that window grows with the font. The finish time no longer shows a date in 1901. Escape clears a selection. Opening the parent folder keeps that folder selected. Find starts in the folder you have open. Get Info can be resized, and it says so when an item cannot be renamed. Open With remembers its size.

The leaf menu says Shut down, with the same words as the question. An empty Recent menu stays on the screen. Escape closes an open menu. A vertical Deskbar does not shrink below one icon row.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10018 - OpenGL
OpenGL drawing uses Mesa 25. A demo such as GLTeapot still draws on the processor, not on the graphics chip. The older Mesa 22 package is no longer on this disk.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10017 - License links
The Licenses tab uses the same green headings as What's new. It links to the license pages, and lists the same components as Credits, including their license text.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10016 - Licenses
About has a Licenses tab, next to Credits and What's new. Credits stays the list of people and projects.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10015 - Window size and shared folders
The disk has more free space. On VirtualBox, the desktop follows the window when you resize it. A folder the host shares shows up on the Desktop as VBox Share. On another computer those stay off.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10014 - VirtualBox guest
On VirtualBox, the desktop can follow the window size. A shared folder can be mounted when the host offers one. This build includes GNU GPL version 3 code: the VirtualBox guest software. The license and the matching source are stored on this disk with the other license texts.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10013 - Lock screen
Lock screen in the leaf menu covers the screen. The lock window has the same title tab as Sign in. Your apps stay open. Type your own password to continue. Log out on that screen returns to Sign in. A preference window that was unlocked locks again.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10012 - Shared machine settings
Printers, Repositories, and VirtualMemory stay in Preferences for everyone. An administrator can change them. Another account uses the lock at the bottom right. Printer queues and swap belong to the computer. A new account needs a password. The account that is signed in cannot be removed. Adding an account no longer shows an error after it has succeeded.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10011 - Your account
Users & Accounts opens on your own card: picture, name, username, email, and password. Other people stay hidden until an administrator unlocks the list. Sign in still shows everyone who can sign in.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10010 - Log out name, Sign in after Restart
The leaf menu says Log out and your account name. Restart and a cold boot go to Sign in again after the first administrator is no longer user id 0.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10009 - Locked machine settings
Network, Screen, Time, and Users & Accounts stay in Preferences for everyone. An administrator can change them. Another account can look, then open the lock at the bottom right with an administrator's name and password. Until then the buttons in that window stay off. That lasts a few minutes and is checked by Sign in. Appearance and the other personal settings stay with the person who is signed in.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10008 - Sign in as that person
Sign in starts the desktop as the account you chose. A standard account uses its own home under home/users and its own Tracker. Terminal uses that same home. Trash, Home, Quick Tour, and User Guide are on that desktop. Folder icons in Tracker and the leaf menu (Applications, Demos, Desktop applets, Preferences) are the same yellow folders for everyone. Each account has its own MIME database. The administrator still uses /boot/home. There is still one screen. Preferences are not hidden per role yet.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10007 - Administrator is not root
After Sign in, the first account (`user`, home folder `/boot/home`) is a normal user id (1000 or higher), not 0. Tracker and Deskbar run as that person. Another account’s folder under home/users stays unreadable. Sign in as someone else still opens this same desktop for now. Users & Accounts still works: the sign-in program applies the changes.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10006 - Private home folders
Another account cannot open your Desktop or Documents. Extra people still live under home/users. The original account is user id 0, so that one can still read the others. Everyone still shares one desktop after Sign in.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10005 - Log out
The leaf menu can log you out. Tracker, Deskbar, and other session apps close, then Sign in comes back. The next Sign in is still the same computer session — homes are not isolated yet.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10004 - Sign in
The desktop waits at a sign-in screen. Choose a person, type the password, and press Sign in. Restart and Shut down sit at the bottom. An empty password still works if the account has none. This is a gate, not separate desktops: after sign-in you still get the same session until logout and isolation exist.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10003 - Add and remove accounts
Users & Accounts can add a person, disable an account, or delete it. Open it from Preferences. New people get a user id of 1000 or higher and a folder under home/users. System accounts such as sshd stay off the list. The original administrator cannot be removed. An empty password is allowed; that is convenient, not a lock. Everyone still shares the same desktop until a login screen exists.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10002 - Users & Accounts
Preferences now includes Users & Accounts. It lists the accounts that already exist on this computer (name, user id, home folder, and whether the account looks like an administrator). You cannot yet add people, set passwords, log in as someone else, or keep separate desktops. Everyone still shares the same session.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - IPv6
On a home network the computer can take an IPv6 address from the router as well as IPv4, and use that address to reach the rest of the network. Open Network, select the card, and look under IPv6. Automatic is the default. You should see a link-local address and, if the router offers one, a second address. Set IPv6 to Disabled if you do not want that.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - Shutdown
Shutting down should no longer sit forever while IPv6 autoconfig (SLAAC) is still listening. If it still hangs, say so — ACPI power-off is a different path.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - AirPlay
We've added AirPlay so you can play this computer's sound on speakers on your Wi-Fi network, such as Sonos. Open the AirPlay icon in the Deskbar, pick a speaker, and choose Play to. You can also receive classic AirPlay 1 audio on this computer. An iPhone usually will not list R5OS in Control Center yet — AirPlay 2 pairing is not included.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - Wi-Fi
Wi-Fi is on one screen: scan, enter a password, and connect. Open it from Network preferences or from the network icon in the Deskbar. Networks you join are remembered, and R5OS can reconnect after sleep or a dropped link. The Deskbar tooltip shows the network name, or that it is still connecting.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - AirPrint
You can add network printers that speak AirPrint or IPP. Open the printer settings, add a printer, and choose one that appears on the network. This is new; not every printer has been tried.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - Dark Mode
You can switch the desktop to a dark look. Open Preferences → Appearance, choose a dark color scheme, and apply it. More of the system (Deskbar, Tracker, windows) follows those colors than before. Some apps still keep their own look.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - Desktop and Deskbar
Icons on the Desktop overlap less, sit clearer of the Deskbar, and keep a sensible size. The Deskbar clock, mute, battery, and menus behave more predictably. Lists, scrollbars, and alert windows clip and resize more cleanly.

R5OS 2026.1-dev Build 10001 - Name and About
The system identifies as R5OS in the Deskbar, installer, boot splash, and About R5OS. Open About R5OS from the Deskbar leaf menu. This is not an official Haiku release.
